<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mesopotamia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mesopotamian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mesopotamia is the region between and around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> rivers, mainly in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. It was the setting of the earliest cities, writing and empires, including the Akkadian, Neo-Sumerian, Babylonian and Assyrian empires. Its rich agricultural land later made it central to the Achaemenid, Seleucid, Parthian, Sasanian and Abbasid states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mesopotamia.jpg" alt="Mesopotamia" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Goran tek-en · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N-Mesopotamia_and_Syria_english.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The caliphate's revenues derived from agricultural taxation across <b>Mesopotamia</b>'s highly productive Sawad, customs duties on the Indian Ocean trade passing through the Persian Gulf, and remittances from provincial governors across an empire stretching from North Africa to Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Achaemenid Empire was one of antiquity's most economically sophisticated polities, ruling a large proportion of the world's population (modern estimates vary widely) and drawing revenues from the most productive agricultural regions of the ancient world: <b>Mesopotamia</b>, Egypt and the Levant.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Over the course of what his inscriptions describe as 34 battles, Sargon brought the Sumerian city-states of southern <b>Mesopotamia</b> under Akkadian control, symbolically washing his weapons in the Lower Sea, the Persian Gulf, after the conquest of the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Agriculture in the river valleys of <b>Mesopotamia</b> and the plains of Azerbaijan and western Iran produced most of the state’s revenue, collected as land tax and as the tamgha, a tax on trade and urban crafts inherited from the Mongol period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Assyrian Empire's origins trace back to the city of Assur on the west bank of the Tigris in northern <b>Mesopotamia</b>, which emerged as an independent city-state around 2025 BCE following the collapse of the Third Dynasty of Ur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He took Damascus that autumn, progressively absorbed the Zengid principalities of Syria and northern <b>Mesopotamia</b> through a combination of military pressure and diplomacy, sent his brother Turanshah to conquer Yemen in 1174, securing the Red Sea route, took Aleppo in 1183 and received the submission of Mosul in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
